    "text": "Let $x$ be the integer we are looking for. We want to find the integer $x$ such that $x^2 < 2x$.\n\nRearranging the inequality, we have $x^2 - 2x < 0$.\n\nFactoring out an $x$, we get $x(x - 2) < 0$.\n\nThe product of two numbers is negative if and only if one number is positive and the other is negative.\n\nSo, we have two cases to consider:\n\nCase 1: $x > 0$ and $x - 2 < 0$.\n\nIn this case, we have $x > 0$ and $x < 2$. The only integer that satisfies both conditions is $x = 1$.\n\nCase 2: $x < 0$ and $x - 2 > 0$.\n\nIn this case, we have $x < 0$ and $x > 2$. There are no integers that satisfy both conditions.\n\nTherefore, the only integer whose square is less than its double is $\\boxed{1}$.The answer is: 1",
